      <path d="M 0 1 L 8 5 L 0 9 z" fill="#334155"/>
    </marker>
  </defs>
</svg>
</div>
<p>Which option correctly identifies the mutated mRNA codon <strong>[ P ]</strong> and the substituted amino acid residue <strong>[ Q ]</strong> at the sixth position of the mutant \(\beta\)-globin chain?</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1: Point Mutation at the DNA Level</strong><br/>In sickle-cell anaemia, a single base substitution occurs in the gene coding for the \(\beta\)-globin chain of haemoglobin. In the template DNA strand at the sixth codon, the base adenine is substituted by thymine, converting the triplet from CTC to CAC (coding strand GAG to GTG).</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Transcription of Mutated Codon [ P ]</strong><br/>Transcription of the mutant DNA sequence results in an mRNA carrying the codon GUG instead of the normal codon GAG at the sixth position. Thus, [ P ] is GUG.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Translation and Substituted Amino Acid [ Q ]</strong><br/>The codon GAG codes for glutamic acid (Glu), which is hydrophilic. The mutated codon GUG codes for valine (Val), which is hydrophobic. Consequently, glutamic acid at the sixth position is replaced by valine. Thus, [ Q ] is Val.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Consequence on RBC Morphology</strong><br/>Under low oxygen tension, the mutant haemoglobin (\(Hb^S\)) molecules polymerise into fibrous precipitates, distorting the normal biconcave erythrocyte into an elongated sickle-shaped cell.</p>
<p><strong>Final Answer:</strong><br/>The mutated mRNA codon [ P ] is GUG, and the substituted amino acid [ Q ] is Valine (Val).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
