    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1: Establishing Recessive Inheritance</strong><br/>In Generation I, both parents (I-1 and I-2) are phenotypically unaffected, yet they produce affected offspring in Generation II (male II-2 and female II-5). Similarly, unaffected parents II-3 and II-4 have an affected child (III-1). When unaffected parents produce affected progeny, the condition skips generations, establishing that the trait is recessive.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Establishing Autosomal vs. Sex-Linked Recessive Inheritance</strong><br/>In Generation II, an affected female (II-5) is born to an unaffected father (I-1). If the trait were X-linked recessive, an affected female would require an affected father (\(X^a Y\)) carrying the mutant allele. Because the father is unaffected, the trait cannot be X-linked recessive. Furthermore, both males and females are affected in equal frequencies, confirming autosomal recessive inheritance.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Determining Genotypes of Parents in Generation I</strong><br/>For two unaffected parents to produce homozygous recessive affected children (\(aa\)), both parents must carry one copy of the recessive mutant allele while expressing the normal dominant phenotype. Therefore, both parents in Generation I must be heterozygous carriers with genotype \(Aa\).</p>
<p><strong>Final Answer:</strong><br/>The trait is autosomal recessive, and the genotypes of both parents in Generation I are \(Aa\) and \(Aa\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
